8) app/Models/ -

	This is where Eloquent models live.

	A model is not simply a representation of a database table.  

	An Eloquent model can contain:

	i)relationships - 
		| Laravel            | Meaning                              | MySQL structure                 |
		| ------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------- |
		| `hasOne()`         | One-to-one                           | Foreign key in related table    |
		| `hasMany()`        | One-to-many                          | Foreign key in related table    |
		| `belongsTo()`      | Child → parent                       | Foreign key in current table    |
		| `belongsToMany()`  | Many-to-many                         | Pivot table                     |
		| `hasManyThrough()` | One → many through another model     | JOIN through intermediate table |
		| `morphMany()`      | One model → many polymorphic records | `*_id` + `*_type`               |
		| `morphTo()`        | Polymorphic child → any parent type  | `*_id` + `*_type`               |
		
		A model can describe how one piece of data is related to another.
		For example:

		A User has many Orders
		An Order belongs to a User
		A Post belongs to a User
		A Post has many Comments

		Eloquent: $user->orders
		is basically giving you a convenient PHP interface over database operations such as:
		SELECT *
		FROM orders
		WHERE user_id = 1;


		Common relationship types

		1)hasOne() - One User has one Profile.
		users - 

		| id | name  |
		| -: | ----- |
		|  1 | John  |
		|  2 | Sarah |

		profiles - 

		| id | user_id | bio       |
		| -: | ------: | --------- |
		| 10 |       1 | Developer |
		| 11 |       2 | Designer  |

		profiles.user_id
		       ↑
		       └── points to users.id

		In User.php:

		public function profile()
		{
		    return $this->hasOne(Profile::class);
		}

		$user = User::find(1);
		$user->profile;

		(SELECT *
		FROM profiles
		WHERE user_id = 1
		LIMIT 1;)


		2)hasMany() -
		app/Models/User.php:
		<?php

		namespace App\Models;

		use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

		class User extends Model
		{
		public function orders()
		{
		return $this->hasMany(Order::class);
		}
		}
		"A User has many Order records."
			
