ii) casts - 
		A cast tells Eloquent how a database attribute should be converted when you use it in PHP.
		For example, MySQL might store:
		is_active = 1
		But in PHP, you want: true

		You tell Laravel:
		protected $casts = [
		    'is_active' => 'boolean',
		];
		 
		 Common casts
		 
		 protected $casts = [
		    'is_active' => 'boolean',  // Database: 1 PHP: true
		    'age' => 'integer', // Database: "25" PHP: 25
		    'price' => 'decimal:2', // Database: 500 PHP: 500.00
		    'settings' => 'array',
		    'metadata' => 'json',
		    'birth_date' => 'date',
		    'created_at' => 'datetime',
		];

iii) scopes -   A scope in Laravel Eloquent is a reusable way to define a common query condition.
		for eg - Whenever I want active users, I don't want to write where('is_active', 1) again and again. 

		Suppose your users table has:
		id
		name
		email
		is_active

		You might repeatedly write:
		$users = User::where('is_active', 1)->get();

		Another controller:
		$users = User::where('is_active', 1)
			     ->orderBy('name')
			     ->get();
			     
		$users = User::where('is_active', 1)
			     ->orderBy('name')
			     ->get();
				          
		$count = User::where('is_active', 1)->count();        

		Scope + other conditions     
		Scopes are especially useful because you can continue building the query.

		Suppose:

		public function scopeActive($query)
		{
		    return $query->where('is_active', 1);
		}

		You can do:

		$users = User::active()
			     ->where('country', 'India')
			     ->get();

		Multiple scopes

		class User extends Model
		{
		    public function scopeActive($query)
		    {
			return $query->where('is_active', 1);
		    }

		    public function scopeVerified($query)
		    {
			return $query->where('email_verified', 1);
		    }

		    public function scopeAdults($query)
		    {
			return $query->where('age', '>=', 18);
		    }
		}

		Now you can combine them:
		$users = User::active()
			     ->verified()
			     ->adults()
			     ->get();
			     
		Conceptually:
		SELECT *
		FROM users
		WHERE is_active = 1
		  AND email_verified = 1
		  AND age >= 18;


iv) accessors -


		attribute behavior - An accessor controls what happens when you read an attribute from an Eloquent model.

		Accessor = change or calculate a value when you GET/read it.

		For example: $user->name

		Normally Laravel gives you the name value directly from the database.
		An accessor lets you say:
		“Whenever someone reads name, I want Laravel to modify it first.”

		example:
		users
		--------------------------------
		id | name
		--------------------------------
		1  | john

		$user = User::find(1);

		echo $user->name;

		Output: john

		Now imagine you want the name displayed in uppercase.
		You can define an accessor in the model.

		use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Casts\Attribute;

		class User extends Model
		{
		    protected function name(): Attribute
		    {
			return Attribute::make(
			    get: fn ($value) => strtoupper($value),
			);
		    }
		}

		Now:
		$user = User::find(1);

		echo $user->name;

		outputs:JOHN

		But the database still contains the same john.
		The accessor changed the value you get in PHP, not the database value.



v) mutators - The opposite of an accessor, Accessor Controls reading, Mutator Controls writing
		Accessor = GET  cannot change the value in the database.
		Mutator  = SET  i.e mutator can change the value in the database.

		protected function name(): Attribute
		{
		    return Attribute::make(
			get: fn ($value) => strtoupper($value),

			set: fn ($value) => strtolower($value),
		    );
		}

				    Eloquent Model
				         │
			  ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
			  ↓              ↓              ↓
			Scope           Cast         Accessor
			  │              │              │
			  ↓              ↓              ↓
		      Query logic     Data type      Read logic
		      
		A very common use: password hashing

		One classic use of a mutator is transforming a password before storing it.
		use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Casts\Attribute;
		use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Hash;

		class User extends Model
		{
		    protected function password(): Attribute
		    {
			return Attribute::make(
			    set: fn ($value) => Hash::make($value),
			);
		    }
		}

		Then your application can do:
		$user->password = 'my-secret-password';

		$user->save();

		The mutator transforms the password before storage.
		"my-secret-password"
			↓
		     mutator
			↓
		    Hash::make()
			↓
		"hashed value..."
			↓
		     database


