vii)query behavior- 
		Suppose you write: $query = User::where('is_active', 1); The database hasn't necessarily been queried yet.
		Then:
		$users = $query->get(); causes the query to execute.

		get() - executes the query and returns an Eloquent Collection.
		first() - first() gets the first matching model.
		find() - find() is designed to find a model by its primary key.
		find()
		 ↓
		"Find this exact primary-key record."

		first()
		 ↓
		"Give me the first record matching this query."
		all() - Give me all users.
		where() - where() adds a condition to the query. User::where('age', '>=', 18) 
		Multiple where() conditions
		$users = User::where('is_active', 1)
			     ->where('age', '>=', 18)
			     ->get();
			     
		orderBy() - You can control ordering:$users = User::orderBy('name')->get();

		limit() - $users = User::limit(10)->get(); You can limit how many records are returned:

		count() -  If you only want the number of matching records:$count = User::where('is_active', 1)->count(); SELECT COUNT(*)
		FROM users
		WHERE is_active = 1;
		exists() -   If you only care whether at least one matching record exists: $exists = User::where('email', $email)->exists();
		The result is: true or false.
		create() - Now we're moving from reading to writing.
		Suppose: 
		$user = User::create([
		    'name' => 'John',
		    'email' => 'john@example.com',
		]);
		Eloquent creates a new model and inserts it into the database.
		INSERT INTO users (name, email)
		VALUES ('John', 'john@example.com');
		For create() to work with mass assignment, the relevant attributes must be allowed by the model's mass-assignment configuration.
		// Find by primary key
		$user = User::find(1);

		// Find or 404/exception
		$user = User::findOrFail(1);

		// Get many
		$users = User::where('is_active', 1)->get();

		// Get one or null
		$user = User::where('email', $email)->first();

		// Check existence
		$exists = User::where('email', $email)->exists();

		// Count
		$count = User::where('is_active', 1)->count();

		// Create
		$user = User::create($data);

		// Change
		$user->name = 'Sarah';

		// Check unsaved changes
		$user->isDirty('name');

		// See changed attributes
		$user->getDirty();

		// See original value
